From 62ef2098bc6b0776cfd83fda4c44c482b4d4425c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Michael C. Ferguson" <michael.christopher.ferguson@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 20:31:45 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] usb: dwc2: host: acknowledge a SOF with nothing due in
 hard IRQ context

In buffer DMA mode the host unmasks the SOF interrupt as long as any
periodic QH is scheduled, so the handler runs every (micro)frame: 8000
times a second behind a high-speed hub. Most of those passes find no
periodic QH due and no QH waiting for a channel, and do nothing. With
threaded interrupts, as on PREEMPT_RT, each of them still wakes the IRQ
thread and costs two context switches.

Have the HCD publish, under hsotg->lock, what the next SOF has to do:
a "kick" when a non-periodic QH is inactive, a periodic QH is ready or
assigned but has no channel yet (dwc2_sof_intr() selects transactions
for both schedules, not only the periodic one) or an inactive periodic
QH is already due, else the next_active_frame of the inactive periodic
QH nearest to the current frame. It is updated at the end of every HCD
pass, when a QH is added or deactivated, and after the NAK wait timer
has run. dwc2_hcd_hold_sof() reads it without the lock and, when SOF is
the only pending interrupt and nothing is due by the current
(micro)frame, clears the SOF itself and reports it handled. It then
reads HFNUM back, which flushes the write before the line is unmasked;
if a new frame began meanwhile, the SOF it may have cleared is handed
to the thread instead. One SOF in 64 is always let through, so a missed
update costs at most 64 frames of latency (8 ms behind a high-speed
port, 64 ms at full speed) instead of a stall, and is counted.

On PREEMPT_RT a host-only core now requests its line with a hard
primary handler and IRQF_ONESHOT, as forced threading would set, so the
line is masked and unmasked as before; the thread disables bottom
halves around the handler as the forced thread does. Dual-role and
peripheral cores keep the plain request: the gadget's handler on the
same line would be force-threaded and wake its thread on every SOF,
which would cancel the saving. Elsewhere the check runs at the top of
the plain handler, which saves the SOF pass and its
select_transactions() call. The primary returns IRQ_NONE when nothing
is pending, rather than waking the thread for an empty pass.

The SOF mask is untouched, so urb_enqueue() still reads GINTMSK.SOF to
decide whether to select transactions inline. Descriptor DMA, a
suspended or hibernated port and device mode are passed through. While
the hold-off is on, held SOFs are not reported as missed ones by
dwc2_track_missed_sofs().

The hold-off is on by default and can be switched off per controller at
run time through a "sof_holdoff" debugfs file. Its decisions are counted
in "hcd_stats".

This needs "usb: dwc2: host: serve the HCD from the common interrupt
handler", which leaves one action on the line: with the HCD's own
usb_hcd_irq() action also there, its forced thread would still wake on
every SOF. It builds on the counters added by the previous patch.

Signed-off-by: Michael C. Ferguson <michael.christopher.ferguson@gmail.com>
